A||||5||yo_sweet||adding another hard drive to your pc?||||1||
Z||000000||yo_sweet||10-15-2001||11:30 PM||||i would like to add another hard drive from my 486 to my pentium for extra space is it just plug and play or do i need a driver?<BR><P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero SE<BR>customized tail light's,american raceing rim's,k&n air filter,pioneer cd player.. more to come<P>kill's: 1990 Honda CRX,93 ACURA integra, 85 mr2,94 pontiac grand am<P>loses: 1996 camero Z28||||reg||1
Z||000001||dennis_6||10-15-2001||11:46 PM||||No driver, but not quite plug and play.  You have to set them for Master and Slave.  The one out of the 486 should be set for slave.  You will probaly have a jumper that has to be set in the slave position for the 486 HDD.<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000002||yo_sweet||10-15-2001||11:54 PM||||coaul you explain how to do that||||reg||1
Z||000003||dennis_6||10-16-2001||12:08 AM||||Take you hard drive out of the 486.  Look at the back there should be a jumper that is near where the ribbon cable plugs in.  There should be three settings: master, slave, and cable select.  If it does not say which is what, you will have to look up the manufactures website and look for the tech info on the drive.  You will also have to enter you bios and tell it to auto detect a slave drive.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||10-16-2001||12:51 AM||||What kind of PC is the 2nd Hard Drive going into?  It could be simply Plug N Play, if you're lucky. Otherwise, you will have to manually set jumpers.  Any modern PC will recognize a HDD from a 486, but I'd recommend putting it on the 2nd IDE channel since it's undoubetly lower performance than the HDD in your Pentium PC.  Assuming you don't have a CDROM, or your CDROM is on the same ribbon cable as your HDD, all you have to do is plug a new ribbon cable into the 486 HDD and plug it into the "Secondary IDE" plug on your motherboard.  It should already be set to master if it was the only HDD in the 486.  <P>If you run into any snags, just give a holler!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Steve Normington||10-17-2001||10:46 AM||||It is a 2.5 or 2.8 Gig drive? You can swap a 3.4 Gig for a 2.8 Gig after drilling the mounting holes, or a 3.0 SD for the 2.5 Gig. The 3.0 SD is rated to 10,000 rpm instead of 7,200 rpm for the 2.5, 2,8 or 3.4 Gig.||||reg||1
